    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d753">

                <pb facs="1037" n="1027" sameAs="frus:frus1946v10/pg1027"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Marshall Mission Files, Lot 54–D270</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by General Marshall to Colonel Marshall S.
                            Carter
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">OSE 610</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-11-30">30 November
                        1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Attached memorandum prepared at the Embassy’s request by Mr. Boiling
                        Powell<anchor n="81" xml:id="d753-f1"/> outlines in general various
                    considerations connected with the loan CNAC has been attempting to obtain
                    through the Eximbank. It is suggested that you hand this to appropriate Eximbank
                    official for their use in considering the CNAC project.</p>
                <p>Any loan of this nature, General Marshall thinks, should be based by the Eximbank
                    on the commercial and technical aspects.</p>
                <p>The restoration or expansion and improvement of communications in China—water,
                    rail and air—is of fundamental importance to the economic recovery of the
                    country. The problem is just how much of any loan should be granted in the light
                    of the present political and military attitude of the government. General
                    Marshall previously stated that he thought preliminary loans might well be
                    granted for the restoration of the Canton–Hankow railroad and the first year
                    preparations for the Yellow River bridge project. For the same reason a
                    preliminary loan of about 10 million might be granted to initiate the
                    development of CNAC, providing the commercial and technical (CAB<anchor n="82"
                        xml:id="d753-f2"/> problems) appeared to justify such action.</p>
                <closer>For General Marshall: <lb/>
                    <signed>J. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hart Caughey</hi>
                    </signed>
                    <lb/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Colonel, G. S. C.</hi>
                </closer>



                <xi:include href="d753-a1.xml" xpointer="d753-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
